  "finalReply": "Done. `/app/steal.py` recovers `A1` by black-box queries only.\n\nMethod: `f(x)=A2·ReLU(A1·x+b1)+b2` is piecewise-linear; crossing hyperplane `w_i·x+b_i=0` jumps the gradient by `±a2_i·w_i`:\n- Scan random lines (`base+ t·d`, `t∈[-10,10]`, 4000 samples) for kinks via `|f[i-1]-2f[i]+f[i+1]|>1e-8`.\n- Localize each kink by bisection to `1e-9`, take central-difference gradients (`eps=1e-6`) at `±2e-4` along the line, difference = scaled row of `A1`.\n- Deduplicate by `|cosine|>0.99` (same neuron, sign/scale invariant), repeat lines until 15 lines yield nothing new (max 80).\n\nVerification: on the given `forward.py` (20×10) it recovers all 20 rows with `min |cos|=1.000000` (bijective match), saved to `/app/stolen_A1.npy`; also verified on synthetic 5/8/30-neuron models at scales 0.1–1.0. Running `python3 /app/steal.py` prints the matrix and writes `/app/stolen_A1.npy` (20×10).",
